/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303770}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303049} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc60d9e6-e722-530f-815d-f6e9699ad521" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304677} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222e20ac-628a-52cc-b6e2-0740bf1ed111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302669} {:answer/id #uuid "9608daff-c66c-54e1-9461-bfdfb2cee161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303389}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302669} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff79bc90-0eb0-5db0-8d87-1e03f2cdf810" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5510f723-c114-5fc6-897e-1de8385b1619" :question/math-academy-id "q-332743" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[2,\\text{ }2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=4,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y &={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =3,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=4,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y &=8t{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.79$ | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | $-0.89$ | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76c7d46c-c2d8-59e5-a29f-80ef79277a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303186} {:answer/id #uuid "775facfc-4bcb-5218-ab04-a17f552d4431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303192} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3e9b53-7d94-5035-b100-d2456ce60c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303349} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa99251-4053-59d5-92cc-1099b003838e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303352} {:answer/id #uuid "a155c759-1dde-55ea-8aef-b139ed04e421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303452} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaab312d-cda1-5b9e-8419-3e89d9fc3c59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a64ea70-c92d-5f84-84d5-defa1ae3d2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-332745" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also constant, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b4344f-0e6c-5645-9574-a4c4a3c9788b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358122} {:answer/id #uuid "39037208-51e5-5863-918d-3b38c718efe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358255} {:answer/id #uuid "5862a07a-00c2-5fcb-8b00-8455797aff2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358517} {:answer/id #uuid "7c71711f-2a1a-589c-970a-8f9a25f4171d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358829} {:answer/id #uuid "97071b96-0058-5668-ab53-95d833ab5cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358517} :answer-field/id #uuid "60925d35-5561-5000-bd1c-d7c04cb2463d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d801e9d7-50c3-5a8d-84f4-42fa2bf6b19c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96957" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨(0-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)),-(0-(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)),(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb6daa8-47d1-51ec-a600-b0e77bdaf8f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}⟨-3,2,\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357849} {:answer/id #uuid "3efe6941-66fe-5623-9925-d4c3445ff507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358290} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0e5b7f-777a-57ae-9f96-4bd2b30e3841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359138} {:answer/id #uuid "ba45b7f5-898c-5689-9b5e-619b196c5938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}⟨-1,-1,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359370} {:answer/id #uuid "c2871b39-7159-5080-b145-ef6116ae9ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨-2,4,\\sqrt{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359138} :answer-field/id #uuid "3739dced-1eee-531b-bfa1-15e98d7e137c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a5b8fb4d-5823-5562-9f22-dd9c5d6ae67a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96958" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=2$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,-1,2⟩, \\\\ N(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(2) & =T(2)\\times N(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,-1,2⟩\\times \\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨(-2-4),-(4+2),(4-1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨-6,-6,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bba9888-420b-5177-9749-b2a2df95fa99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357850} {:answer/id #uuid "5321b4d9-3b60-50aa-86d2-67d71264b404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨2,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358469} {:answer/id #uuid "6169e31e-bcf7-5263-9af6-da6e185aa542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358599} {:answer/id #uuid "96846c65-c6eb-5c00-b01c-0fde168b7d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359068} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aee3fc-9b7d-5c2c-938d-0b42025a181c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357850} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc6b7c4a-1590-5bf4-bd80-56a591de4f1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "553afed7-053a-5b06-8cb8-cd7b8f67d1b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96959" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨({sin}^{2}⁡t-(-{cos}^{2}⁡t)),-(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28828bd8-ec03-59b4-8d26-8b3e464c2850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485833} {:answer/id #uuid "58f62199-efbb-5003-b38f-1311c44d694c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485922} {:answer/id #uuid "b6de3b5d-312f-5e9a-a40a-688e667d53b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486094} {:answer/id #uuid "d14bae15-857b-5c9b-9d59-85235b09badf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486146} {:answer/id #uuid "f1913509-00f4-5dea-ba75-f27bf7624bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485833} :answer-field/id #uuid "6706dd19-f83f-5a69-b331-b8b66eafd0da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "38d9f4a8-a08e-535a-9477-a86a4bf7e353" :question/math-academy-id "q-99414" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩, \\\\ N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(0) & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨(0-1),-(0-(-1)),(1-(-1))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,-1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "655616af-26c7-5045-9c00-22ddde224244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361294} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4f75ae-d6ad-5a3c-91cb-30e81c920d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨0,1,t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361390} {:answer/id #uuid "991991bb-1478-585f-92cd-d50366a4e536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨-2,t,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361520} {:answer/id #uuid "b70d3730-f7e4-550d-8e7c-aaa70734dfad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361653} {:answer/id #uuid "db90d517-2caf-5377-83c7-89e38b72b976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361814} :answer-field/id #uuid "4efaf2f0-5db1-5f94-9975-e72a5434ef99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "38a9961c-14c0-5e12-92b6-0fd05f67f8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-99504" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+{(-2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{16+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}⟨4,\\,-2t,\\,0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & -t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-{t}^{2}-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0,\\,0,\\,-({t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171711 :question/id #uuid "fce43073-3f48-5e12-a5c0-ecef3a31a57a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12346" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} (x-y)\\: \\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx = u+v, \\qquad \ny = u-v, \\qquad \nz = v\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =x-y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)-(u-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2v\\sqrt{6}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171712 :question/id #uuid "8ed57b1f-0082-5281-b061-ff2020ed7d66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12364" :question/problem "The fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=e^{x/2}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in [0,2]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\big\\langle \\text{_______}\\,, \\:-e^{x/2} \\cos\\theta, \\: -e^{x/2}\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWhat is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=e^{x/2} \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=e^{x/2},$ and \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=e^{x/2},$ we obtain $f' = \\dfrac12 e^{x/2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,-{e}^{x/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{e}^{x/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $1$st component of the product is $\\dfrac{1}{2}e^x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171713 :question/id #uuid "a2ff0c76-0d26-51a2-a2fb-498ff5f85559" :question/math-academy-id "e-12365" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to find an integral expression for the surface area generated when the curve $x=\\sin z$ is rotated by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis for $z \\in [0, \\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\sin z \\geq 0$ for all $z \\in [0, \\pi].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\sin z,$ and\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (z,\\theta) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi , \\, 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\sin z,$ we obtain $f'(z) = \\cos z.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171866 :question/id #uuid "78a80655-36ca-5628-b11a-4ac492b7cb3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8938" :question/problem "Compute a normal vector to the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=u, \\quad \ny=1+u^2, \\quad\nz=v^2\n$$\n\nat the point where $u=1$ and $v=2.$" :question/worked-solution "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: 1 + u^2, \\: v^2\\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2u,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,2) & =⟨1,\\,2(1),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,2) & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point where $u=1$ and $v=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,2)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,2) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,-4,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171867 :question/id #uuid "da389dec-eca9-5f6c-8487-fdcbc0cca227" :question/math-academy-id "e-8939" :question/problem "Compute a normal vector to the parametric surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)= \\big\\langle u^2-2, \\: u-v, \\: v^3+1 \\big\\rangle$ at the point $(-1,2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $(-1,2,0)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{u}^{2} - 2 = - 1 \\\\ u - v = 2 \\\\ {v}^{3} + 1 = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}u = 1 \\\\ v = - 1.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{\\prime } ( u , v ) & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  u}} , \\hspace{0.222em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  u}} , \\hspace{0.222em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  z}{\\partial  u}} \\rangle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  u}} ( {u}^{2} - 2 ) , \\hspace{0.222em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  u}} ( u - v ) , \\hspace{0.222em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  u}} ( {v}^{3} + 1 ) \\rangle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  2 u , \\hspace{0.222em} 1 , \\hspace{0.222em} 0 \\rangle  \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{\\prime } ( u , v ) & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  v}} , \\hspace{0.222em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  v}} , \\hspace{0.222em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  z}{\\partial  v}} \\rangle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  v}} ( {u}^{2} - 2 ) , \\hspace{0.222em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  v}} ( u - v ) , \\hspace{0.222em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  v}} ( {v}^{3} + 1 ) \\rangle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  0 , \\hspace{0.222em} - 1 , \\hspace{0.222em} 3 {v}^{2} \\rangle \\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=-1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{\\prime } ( 1 , - 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  2 ( 1 ) , \\hspace{0.222em} 1 , \\hspace{0.222em} 0 \\rangle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  2 , \\hspace{0.222em} 1 , \\hspace{0.222em} 0 \\rangle  \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{\\prime } ( 1 , - 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  0 , \\hspace{0.222em} - 1 , \\hspace{0.222em} 3 ( - 1 {)}^{2} \\rangle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  0 , \\hspace{0.222em} - 1 , \\hspace{0.222em} 3 \\rangle \\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point $(-1,2,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\mathbf{n} & \\displaystyle  = {\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{\\prime } ( 1 , - 1 ) \\times  {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{\\prime } ( 1 , - 1 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}\\displaystyle \\mathbf{i} & \\displaystyle \\mathbf{j} & \\displaystyle \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle - 1 & \\displaystyle 3\\end{array} \\right| \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  \\left| \\begin{array}{cc}\\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle - 1 & \\displaystyle 3\\end{array} \\right| , \\hspace{0.222em} - \\left| \\begin{array}{cc}\\displaystyle 2 & \\displaystyle 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle 3\\end{array} \\right| , \\hspace{0.222em} \\left| \\begin{array}{cc}\\displaystyle 2 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle - 1\\end{array} \\right| \\rangle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\langle  3 , \\hspace{0.222em} - 6 , \\hspace{0.222em} - 2 \\rangle  .\\end{array}\n$$"}]}
